<figure class="traffic-policy-paths" aria-labelledby="traffic-policy-title">
  <figcaption id="traffic-policy-title"><span class="content-kicker">Endpoint eligibility</span><strong>Cluster is a wide pool. Local is a hard boundary. Distribution is a preference.</strong></figcaption>
  <div class="policy-path-grid">
    <article>
      <header><code>trafficPolicy: Cluster</code><span>Reach any eligible endpoint</span></header>
      <div class="node-row"><b>Entry Node</b><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><b>Local Pod</b><i aria-hidden="true">or →</i><b>Remote Pod</b></div>
      <p>Broader distribution; an extra Node hop and source translation may appear on some external paths.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="local-path">
      <header><code>trafficPolicy: Local</code><span>Only this Node’s endpoints</span></header>
      <div class="node-row"><b>Entry Node</b><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><b>Local Pod</b><i aria-hidden="true">·</i><del>Remote Pod</del></div>
      <p>No local endpoint means no eligible endpoint on that Node. External Local can preserve client IP when the surrounding path supports it.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="preference-path">
      <header><code>trafficDistribution</code><span>Prefer topology; allow fallback</span></header>
      <div class="node-row"><b>Client</b><i aria-hidden="true">⇢</i><b>Same zone / Node</b><i aria-hidden="true">↝</i><b>Fallback pool</b></div>
      <p>A preference is not the same as Local. Compatible implementations and safeguards decide when fallback occurs.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</figure>

